          "session_id": "full-mt-m-002",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My doctor says I have type 2 diabetes.",
          "answer": "Your doctor's diagnosis means your body has trouble using insulin well, so your blood sugar (glucose) stays higher than normal. Insulin is the hormone that moves sugar from the blood into the body's cells. In type 2 diabetes, the body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, and the pancreas cannot keep making enough extra to make up for it. It usually develops gradually over years, and many people have no symptoms when it is found. *(Source: Type 2 diabetes mellitus, general clinical reference)*\n\nManagement is usually built around a few areas:\n\n- **Diet.** Cutting back on starchy and sugary foods is common, and sugar-free foods can still contain starch that raises blood sugar. Where body weight is above the healthy range, even a sustained loss of about 5% helps blood sugar, blood pressure and cholesterol. In some people, substantial weight loss can bring blood sugar back to normal without glucose-lowering medication. This is called remission, and it is not a cure, so monitoring continues. *(Source: Diet for type 2 diabetes)*\n- **Physical activity.** Regular activity is part of the guidance. Some complications can change what kind of activity is suitable, so your clinician may assess this with you. *(Source: Physical activity for type 2 diabetes)*\n- **Self-management and follow-up.** Diabetes is largely managed by the person between appointments. Structured diabetes education is described as a core part of care and is usually offered at diagnosis. Regular review is also part of the process. *(Source: Sticking with a diabetes regimen)*\n\nSo what you can expect next is likely a discussion of your blood sugar results, a plan for diet and activity, possibly a referral to diabetes education, and regular check-ups. Your clinician can tell you what applies to you, including any medication decisions. I can't see your results or medical history, so I can't say how your case specifically will be handled. Questions worth bringing to your next appointment include what your test results mean, what targets you should aim for, and whether diabetes education is available to you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
