          "type": "turn",
          "question": "And what should I be eating?",
          "answer": "Here is general dietary guidance for adults. It is not tailored to you. Specific conditions, medications, or goals would change it, so check with your clinician or a dietitian before making big changes.\n\nThe pattern with the most evidence (*Diet for high blood pressure*; *Diet for type 2 diabetes*) is built on whole foods:\n\n- **More:** vegetables, fruit, whole grains, beans and lentils, nuts, fish, poultry, low-fat dairy, and vegetable oils.\n- **Less:** saturated fat (fatty meat, full-fat dairy), added sugars, sugar-sweetened drinks, sweets, and highly processed foods.\n- **Salt:** cutting back on sodium also helps. The effect is larger when combined with the eating pattern above.\n\nSeveral different eating patterns work, including Mediterranean-style, vegetarian, and lower-carbohydrate. No single one is proven best for everyone. The pattern you can keep up with, given your tastes, budget, culture and cooking time, is the one that tends to matter most (*Diet for type 2 diabetes*).\n\nNote that \"sugar-free\" does not mean \"carbohydrate-free.\" Starch raises blood sugar as much as sugar does (*Diet for type 2 diabetes*).\n\nI could not find passages in the corpus that give specific portion sizes, calorie targets, or daily amounts, so I can't offer those.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
